import java.io.Closeable makes that JVM interface available by its short name Closeable. It defines close(). throw IllegalArgumentException() creates and throws an exception, transferring control rather than returning normally. The lambda passed to use runs before close; after runs only after use returns normally. A block failure is propagated after cleanup, so ordinary following code is skipped unless a suitable handler recovers.

This complete failing-path companion constructs IllegalArgumentException with a diagnostic message and throws it inside use. The exception is not a normal return value: use closes the resource before the matching handler prints caught.
```kotlin
import java.io.Closeable
class Trace : Closeable {
    override fun close() { println("close") }
}
fun main() {
    try {
        Trace().use {
            println("body")
            throw IllegalArgumentException("stop")
        }
    } catch (error: IllegalArgumentException) {
        println("caught")
    }
}
```
Expected output: body; close; caught.

